  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Write the vertical translation as a function",
      "body": "<p>Moving a graph upwards by 2 adds 2 to every output. The translated function is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A vertical translation changes the output rather than the input.",
      "pitfall": "This translation does not give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Express the added 2 itself as a logarithm to base 10.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l12",
          "reminder": "A vertical translation changes the output rather than the input."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Connect logarithms with input scaling",
      "body": "Combine the added constant with the logarithm using the product law. Then track an old point: equate the new expression inside the logarithm to its old input.",
      "recap": "Adding logarithms multiplies their positive arguments; a horizontal scale is found by solving for the new coordinate.",
      "pitfall": "The multiplier inside a function is not its horizontal scale factor.",
      "pause": "Rewrite as one logarithm and find the ratio of a new horizontal coordinate to the corresponding old coordinate.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l06",
          "reminder": "Adding logarithms multiplies their positive arguments; a horizontal scale is found by solving for the new coordinate."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1. Translate upwards.</strong> The new graph is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2. Use a logarithm law.</strong> As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>, the new equation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3. Track an old point.</strong> If its old horizontal coordinate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, its height reappears when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so the new horizontal coordinate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The scale factor parallel to the horizontal axis is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer A.</strong> The input multiplier 100 gives a horizontal contraction by 0.01.</p>",
